# Conventional Commit Rules

When creating commits, ALWAYS follow the Conventional Commit standard as defined in `.cursor/commands/conventional-commit.md`.

Refer to that file for the complete specification including:
- Commit message format: `<type>(<scope-branch>): <subject>`
- Type selection (feat, fix, refactor, perf, docs, test, build, ci, chore, style)
- Scope-branch usage (exact branch name from `git rev-parse --abbrev-ref HEAD`)
- Subject formatting rules (imperative, present tense, ≤72 chars)
- Body and footer guidelines
- Behavior rules (check staged files, never push automatically)
